WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.525
Rust helps prevent data races in safe
code,

2
00:00:02.605 --> 00:00:05.025
which is a major advantage for concurrent
systems.

3
00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:08.649
Traits such as Send and Sync express
important thread-safety properties.

4
00:00:08.729 --> 00:00:12.499
They help the compiler decide when values
can move across threads or be shared by

5
00:00:12.579 --> 00:00:13.141
reference.

6
00:00:13.221 --> 00:00:16.710
This makes many unsafe concurrent designs
harder to write accidentally.

7
00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:19.209
Safe Rust still needs careful concurrency
design.

8
00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:22.046
Shared state, locks, channels, atomics,
task lifetimes,

9
00:00:22.126 --> 00:00:25.727
cancellation behavior, timeouts, queues,
and resource limits all matter.

10
00:00:25.807 --> 00:00:27.776
The compiler can prevent many data races,

11
00:00:27.856 --> 00:00:30.950
but it cannot decide whether the chosen
locking strategy is fair,

12
00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:32.606
whether a queue can grow forever,

13
00:00:32.686 --> 00:00:35.386
or whether a task should be cancelled when
a request ends.

14
00:00:35.466 --> 00:00:37.323
Async Rust adds more design questions.

15
00:00:37.403 --> 00:00:41.060
Tasks need clear ownership, timeout
expectations, cancellation behavior,

16
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.153
and error reporting.

17
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:45.946
Blocking operations inside async contexts
can create performance collapse.

18
00:00:46.026 --> 00:00:48.220
Unbounded task growth can harm
availability.

19
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:52.126
A memory-safe service can still fail if it
accepts more work than it can process or

20
00:00:52.206 --> 00:00:54.063
holds resources longer than expected.

21
00:00:54.143 --> 00:00:57.687
Locks and shared resources should be
reviewed for deadlocks, starvation,

22
00:00:57.767 --> 00:00:59.568
and hidden performance bottlenecks.

23
00:00:59.648 --> 00:01:03.080
Backpressure, bounded queues, worker
limits, connection pool limits,

24
00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.410
and graceful degradation are safety
controls.

25
00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:09.653
Memory safety by design includes
protecting reliability because availability is part

26
00:01:09.733 --> 00:01:11.140
of secure software behavior.
